%9Setting:%9

    term_truecolor (choice: auto, on, off; default: auto)

%9Description:%9

    Chooses whether erssi sends 24-bit colours to the terminal.

    AUTO uses 24-bit colours unless the terminal is known not to show them.
    COLORTERM=truecolor or 24bit, and kitty, Ghostty, WezTerm and iTerm2, count
    as 24-bit capable. For any other terminal the 256-colour palette is used
    when:

        - TERM is empty, dumb, linux, ansi or starts with vt,
        - TERM ends in -8color, -16color, -mono or -m,
        - TERM starts with rxvt and does not end in -24bit,
        - the terminal is Apple Terminal.

    Inside GNU Screen (STY is set) the 256-colour palette is always used in
    AUTO mode, whatever the terminal says. ON assumes the terminal shows
    24-bit colours (colors_ansi_24bit must be on too), OFF never sends them.

    ssh does not pass COLORTERM on, so over ssh a capable terminal is
    assumed. If the colours look wrong, set this to OFF.

    The setting works together with colors_ansi_24bit: 24-bit colours are
    sent only when both allow them.

%9Examples:%9

    /SET term_truecolor off
    /SET term_truecolor on

%9See also:%9

    SET, COLORS_ANSI_24BIT
